### What happens when a microservice goes down?

When a downstream microservice goes down, the caller must contain the blast radius so it stays responsive and the failure does not cascade through the system. Without protection, the caller's threads pile up waiting on the dead service, its own callers time out, and the whole chain collapses. The goal is graceful degradation: the system continues to provide core value with reduced functionality rather than failing completely.

```java
package com.bank.transfer;

import io.github.resilience4j.bulkhead.annotation.Bulkhead;
import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.annotation.CircuitBreaker;
import io.github.resilience4j.retry.annotation.Retry;
import io.github.resilience4j.timelimiter.annotation.TimeLimiter;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class FraudCheckService {

    private final FraudServiceClient client;

    public FraudCheckService(FraudServiceClient client) {
        this.client = client;
    }

    @TimeLimiter(name = "fraudTl")
    @CircuitBreaker(name = "fraudCb", fallbackMethod = "fraudFallback")
    @Retry(name = "fraudRetry")
    @Bulkhead(name = "fraudBh")
    public RiskScore checkFraud(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        return client.evaluate(accountId, amount);
    }

    public RiskScore fraudFallback(String accountId, BigDecimal amount,
                                    Throwable t) {
        // Conservative default: flag for manual review
        return new RiskScore("MEDIUM", true);
    }
}
```

```text
Normal flow:  FraudService returns LOW risk -> transfer proceeds
Service down: Circuit breaker opens -> fallback returns MEDIUM + flagged for review
```

### Why is a timeout the most important defense?

A timeout ensures every remote call has a bounded wait. The worst failure is not an error but a call that hangs forever and ties up a thread. In a Spring Boot application using WebClient, you configure response timeouts directly. For Resilience4j, you use the `TimeLimiter` annotation.

```yaml
# application.yml
resilience4j:
  timelimiter:
    instances:
      fraudTl:
        timeout-duration: 2s
        cancel-running-future: true
```

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;
import java.time.Duration;
import io.netty.channel.ChannelOption;
import reactor.netty.http.client.HttpClient;
import org.springframework.http.client.reactive.ReactorClientHttpConnector;

@Configuration
public class WebClientConfig {

    @Bean
    public WebClient fraudWebClient() {
        HttpClient httpClient = HttpClient.create()
            .option(ChannelOption.CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MILLIS, 500)
            .responseTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(2));

        return WebClient.builder()
            .clientConnector(new ReactorClientHttpConnector(httpClient))
            .baseUrl("http://fraud-service")
            .build();
    }
}
```

```text
Without timeout: thread blocked 30+ seconds -> pool exhausted -> gateway down
With 2s timeout: thread freed in 2s -> breaker trips -> fallback served
```

### How does retry with exponential backoff work?

Retry with backoff gives transient failures a second chance without creating a thundering herd. If a call fails, you wait a short, growing delay with a little randomness (jitter) and try again a limited number of times. The key rule is to only retry idempotent operations. Retrying a non-idempotent transfer could double-charge Rajesh.

```yaml
# application.yml
resilience4j:
  retry:
    instances:
      fraudRetry:
        max-attempts: 3
        wait-duration: 200ms
        enable-exponential-backoff: true
        exponential-backoff-multiplier: 2
        enable-randomized-wait: true
        randomized-wait-factor: 0.5
        retry-exceptions:
          - java.io.IOException
          - java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException
```

```text
Retry sequence:
  Attempt 1: immediate         -> TimeoutException
  Attempt 2: wait ~300ms       -> TimeoutException
  Attempt 3: wait ~600ms       -> Success OR circuit breaker trips
```

### What is a circuit breaker and why does it matter?

A circuit breaker stops calling a service that is clearly down. It watches the recent failure rate, and when failures cross a threshold it opens, meaning subsequent calls fail instantly without even attempting the network. This protects both the caller (no wasted threads) and the callee (no pile-on while it recovers). After a cooldown it goes half-open, letting a few trial calls through. If they succeed it closes again; if they fail it reopens.

```yaml
resilience4j:
  circuitbreaker:
    instances:
      fraudCb:
        sliding-window-type: COUNT_BASED
        sliding-window-size: 10
        failure-rate-threshold: 50
        wait-duration-in-open-state: 30s
        permitted-number-of-calls-in-half-open-state: 3
        minimum-number-of-calls: 5
```

```text
State machine:
  CLOSED   -- failure rate >= 50% -->  OPEN
  OPEN     -- 30s cooldown        -->  HALF_OPEN
  HALF_OPEN -- trials pass        -->  CLOSED
  HALF_OPEN -- trials fail        -->  OPEN
```

### What is a bulkhead and why is it needed?

A bulkhead isolates resources so one sick dependency cannot sink the whole ship. Named after watertight compartments in a ship's hull, it limits how many concurrent calls or threads a given dependency may consume. If the statement-generation service becomes slow, a bulkhead caps it at, say, ten concurrent calls, so the remaining threads stay free to serve transfers for Rajesh and Priya.

```yaml
resilience4j:
  bulkhead:
    instances:
      fraudBh:
        max-concurrent-calls: 10
        max-wait-duration: 500ms
```

```java
package com.bank.transfer;

import io.github.resilience4j.bulkhead.annotation.Bulkhead;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class StatementService {

    @Bulkhead(name = "statementBh", fallbackMethod = "statementFallback")
    public String generateStatement(String accountId) {
        // Calls external statement generation
        return statementClient.generate(accountId);
    }

    public String statementFallback(String accountId, Throwable t) {
        return "Statement generation is temporarily unavailable. " +
               "Please try again in a few minutes.";
    }
}
```

```text
Without bulkhead:  slow statement service consumes all 200 threads -> everything slow
With bulkhead:     statement service capped at 10 threads -> 190 threads serve transfers
```

### How does a fallback provide graceful degradation?

A fallback is the graceful answer when the primary path fails. It might return a cached value, a sensible default, or a queued-for-later acknowledgment. For example, if the fraud-scoring service is down, the fallback applies a conservative default risk score and flags the transaction for later review, so the transfer is not simply refused.

```java
package com.bank.transfer;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferOrchestrator {

    private final FraudCheckService fraudCheck;
    private final AccountService accountService;

    public TransferOrchestrator(FraudCheckService fraudCheck,
                                 AccountService accountService) {
        this.fraudCheck = fraudCheck;
        this.accountService = accountService;
    }

    public TransferResult transfer(String from, String to,
                                    BigDecimal amount) {
        // Fraud check returns fallback if service is down
        RiskScore risk = fraudCheck.checkFraud(from, amount);

        if (risk.isHighRisk()) {
            return TransferResult.blocked("High risk transfer blocked");
        }

        // Transfer proceeds even if fraud service was down
        // (flagged for manual review via fallback)
        accountService.debit(from, amount);
        accountService.credit(to, amount);

        return TransferResult.success(
            risk.isFlaggedForReview()
                ? "Transfer completed (pending fraud review)"
                : "Transfer completed"
        );
    }
}
```

```text
Fraud service UP:    LOW risk  -> transfer completes normally
Fraud service DOWN:  MEDIUM risk (fallback) -> transfer completes, flagged for review
Fraud service DOWN:  HIGH amount -> blocked even with fallback (conservative)
```

### How does monitoring close the loop?

Monitoring and alerting tie everything together by turning silent fallbacks into visible signals. Circuit breaker state changes, retry counts, timeout rates, and fallback invocations should emit metrics to Prometheus and alerts to the on-call engineer. A silent fallback that masks an outage for days is its own kind of failure.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.CircuitBreaker;
import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.Health;
import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.HealthIndicator;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class CircuitBreakerHealthIndicator implements HealthIndicator {

    private final CircuitBreaker fraudCb;

    public CircuitBreakerHealthIndicator(CircuitBreaker fraudCb) {
        this.fraudCb = fraudCb;
    }

    @Override
    public Health health() {
        CircuitBreaker.State state = fraudCb.getState();
        if (state == CircuitBreaker.State.OPEN) {
            return Health.down()
                .withDetail("fraudCircuitBreaker", "OPEN")
                .withDetail("failureRate",
                    fraudCb.getMetrics().getFailureRate())
                .build();
        }
        return Health.up()
            .withDetail("fraudCircuitBreaker", state.name())
            .build();
    }
}
```

```text
Prometheus metrics exposed:
  resilience4j_circuitbreaker_state{name="fraudCb"} = 1 (OPEN)
  resilience4j_retry_calls_total{name="fraudRetry",kind="successful_without_retry"}
  resilience4j_bulkhead_available_concurrent_calls{name="fraudBh"}
  resilience4j_timelimiter_calls_total{name="fraudTl",kind="timeout"}
```

### How is this different from gateway-level handling vs service-level handling?

Gateway-level resilience protects the edge, while service-level resilience protects individual dependencies. Both are needed for defense in depth.

| Aspect | Gateway-Level | Service-Level |
|---|---|---|
| Scope | All inbound traffic | Per-dependency calls |
| Tools | Rate limiting, global timeout | Circuit breaker, retry, bulkhead |
| Fallback | Generic error page | Domain-specific cached data |
| Configuration | Centralized | Per-service tuned |
| Example | Kong, Spring Cloud Gateway | Resilience4j annotations |

```text
Defense in depth:
  Client -> [Gateway: rate limit, global timeout]
         -> [Service A: timeout + CB + retry + bulkhead]
            -> [Service B: may also have its own CB + retry]
```
